---
/**
 * REWRITTEN TO SS_WRITING_FOR_KASPAR.md, 26 Nov 2026 (a founder: the remaining
 * area pages rewritten to the style Kaspar comments on; ops rulings D3 and D7).
 * Changed: the opening is one paragraph of two sentences, with no "bespoke
 * carpentry"; each section is headed fitting, room, place, opens with at most
 * one plain line on the kind of homes and then says what we do in that room;
 * the appraisal narration is gone (dates, BAM, the Key flats and leaseholds,
 * listing grades, the Greek Streets' capitals, the Planning Portal rule);
 * the listed homes and parking sections follow K18 and K19; a loft room
 * section added for Lyncroft Gardens; the three FAQ questions kept, answers
 * rewritten to K16. One Ask: the smallest room for a walk-in. Drawings: the
 * three West Hampstead ones stay beside the sections whose first line names
 * their house type; the loft, listed and access sections are plain text, as
 * no West Hampstead drawing shows a loft room. Prices section untouched.
 * FACT LIST (each claim: its source).
 *   - Opening: "design and install" (K10); West End Lane and Fortune Green,
 *     mansion flats and red-brick houses (facts file section 1 and
 *     Distinctive 2: "side streets of red brick houses and mansion blocks");
 *     loft rooms (facts file section 1, Lyncroft Gardens' attic storeys);
 *     planning around alcoves beside a chimney breast, as a condition, not a
 *     claim (his Camden opening, K15).
 *   - Mansion flats above the shops on West End Lane and around Cannon Hill
 *     and Mill Lane: facts file section 1 (S1 5.1 and zone 6).
 *   - A hinged wardrobe often around 600 to 650 mm deep: his wardrobe guide's
 *     starting sizes (built-in-wardrobe-ideas, his 20 Oct text). Brought
 *     forward of the chimney breast in a shallow alcove, exposed sides
 *     finished to match: the same guide ("Wardrobes either side of a chimney
 *     breast"); K21 (alcove wardrobes standing proud of the breast).
 *   - Shaker or handleless doors: facts table, door style and finish are the
 *     customer's choice (23 Nov); his Hampstead mansion flats line (8 Nov).
 *   - Lymington Road, Fawley Road and Crediton Hill, large semi-detached and
 *     detached Edwardian houses: facts file section 1 (S1 zone 5).
 *   - A walk-in designed around the room, box rooms, hanging space, drawers,
 *     shoe storage and shelves planned together: his walk-in page opening
 *     (6 Sep). Finishes to go with flooring and furniture: his walk-in answer
 *     (7 Sep). The smallest room: an Ask, no figure from him.
 *   - Inglewood Road and Agamemnon Road, terraced houses west of the Lane:
 *     facts file section 1 (S1 zones 3 and 4; the Fortune Green streets).
 *   - Shelf gaps of around 250 to 350 mm for books, 350 to 450 mm or more for
 *     larger display pieces: facts table (14 Oct, his alcove guide rewrite).
 *   - Lyncroft Gardens' north side, rooms in the roof: facts file section 1
 *     (S1 zone 4). Hanging space where the ceiling is highest, drawers or
 *     cupboards under the low side: facts table (26 Nov Islington, 23 Nov
 *     Camden). Doors sized to open fully: facts table (drawers behind doors,
 *     23 Nov) and his wardrobe guide ("Check how every door opens beneath the
 *     slope").
 *   - Listed homes: the owner checks, then asks the council about consent,
 *     skirting and panelling: facts table (26 Nov Islington), K18. Camden's
 *     listed buildings page: facts file S4, open in a browser 26 Nov 2026
 *     (200; it links the listed buildings map). A wardrobe below an original
 *     cornice: facts table (10 Nov Hampstead, 23 Nov Camden).
 *   - Parking: where to unload and park agreed before fitting day; zone by
 *     street or postcode; who applies for a permit: facts table (8 and 10 Nov
 *     Hampstead, 23 Nov Camden), K19. Camden's controlled parking zones page
 *     ("Enter a street or postcode") and trade permits page ("Anyone can
 *     apply"): facts file S5, both open in a browser 26 Nov 2026 (200). curl
 *     gets 403 from camden.gov.uk, a bot block, not a dead page.
 *   - Lift, stairs and delivery rules; the managing agent; insurance and
 *     safety documents; normally 8am to 5pm on weekdays, adjusted: facts
 *     table (8 Nov Hampstead). Furniture in sections, assembled in the room:
 *     facts table (10 Nov Hampstead; questionnaire stage 04). Floors and
 *     stairs protected: facts table (23 Nov Camden; conduct fact 6).
 *   - FAQ lighting: his wardrobe guide, "Plan lighting with the joinery".
 *     FAQ alcove: his alcove guide, "Cupboards with shelving above" and its
 *     line on a drawing of both alcoves and the fireplace; drawings where a
 *     project needs detailed design (facts table, 10 Nov Hampstead). FAQ
 *     cost: his wardrobe guide's freestanding answer; "well beyond 10 years
 *     with normal use and care" (facts table, 23 Nov Camden).
 * The notes below describe the 17 Nov text, which this replaces.
 *
 * West Hampstead, 17 November 2026. A draft for Kaspar to read on the review
 * link (F2F ops clients/structure-and-style/areas-review); held noindex by
 * areaWestHampsteadCheck until he has read it and his changes are in. Nothing
 * goes live before he has approved every area page (Denis, 15 Nov 2026).
 *
 * ON THE PRIMROSE HILL MODEL from the start (a founder's go, 17 Nov: "Yes,
 * West Hampstead"; Denis, 15 Nov: "use the same design and structure for the
 * next articles"; ops README, "The structure rule, applied"; ops
 * SS_AREA_PAGE_RULES_v1.0.md, a draft, read for its plan items): address
 * /areas/west-hampstead/ with 301s from /carpenter-west-hampstead/
 * (netlify.toml and the Worker's list); the title, heading, kicker and
 * description lead with fitted wardrobes, then walk-in wardrobes (the reasons
 * are in areas.ts); the opening carries "bespoke carpentry" and "bespoke
 * joinery" once each; a line drawing beside each section that describes
 * something (src/data/area-drawings.ts: a tall gabled mansion block, a large
 * Edwardian house with a balcony over its bay, a terrace of three with carved
 * bay capitals), none where a section is rules or access only; the guide
 * prices as cards; register photos and one labelled AI image (ssp-042 and
 * ssp-044, cropped to 3:2, and AI Shaker alcove wardrobes, Figma node 37:2).
 *
 * Sources. Every local fact from the ops facts file
 * research/area-pages-2026-11-14/facts-west-hampstead-2026-11-14.md, read
 * 14 Nov 2026, mainly Camden's West End Green Conservation Area Appraisal and
 * Management Strategy (adopted 28 Feb 2011, "S1"):
 *   "Mansion blocks are a characteristic of the area"; West End Lane framed
 *     by "5 storey mansion flats over shops" (5.1); Buckingham, Avenue and
 *     Marlborough Mansions, known locally as BAM, tall red brick, turn of the
 *     20th century, around Cannon Hill; Cholmley Gardens, inter-war, wrapping
 *     Hillfield Road, Aldred Road and Mill Lane (zone 6).
 *   East of the Lane (Lymington Road, Fawley Road, Honeybourne Road, Crediton
 *     Hill): large semi-detached and detached "homes for Edwardian
 *     professionals" in red brick, timber and stone balconies, stucco and
 *     terracotta; Crediton Hill's stained-glass oval windows and Arts and
 *     Crafts rendered two-storey houses (zone 5). Yale and Harvard Courts,
 *     Honeybourne Road, built as private rented "Key" flats, leaseholds now
 *     increasingly bought by individuals (zone 5).
 *   West of the Lane: Inglewood Road (1883 to 1884) on a curve; Lyncroft
 *     Gardens' north side, two-storey late Victorian houses raised on
 *     basements with attic storeys (zones 3, 4). West Cottages (1860s, for
 *     foundry workers) beside the Green (zone 2). The Greek Streets near
 *     Fortune Green: two-storey terraces with carved faces on bay window
 *     capitals, named as a possible conservation area of their own.
 *   Listed: Hampstead Synagogue, Dennington Park Road (1892 to 1901, Grade
 *     II*); the fire station, 325 West End Lane (1901, Grade II); Emmanuel
 *     Church (Grade II); the drinking fountain on the Green (Grade II).
 *   Camden's trade permit page; the Planning Portal's internal alterations
 *     page ("S4", "S5").
 * Left out: Article 4 (Camden's page lists South Hampstead but not West End
 * Green; the June 2026 documents could not be read), King's Gardens and the
 * mews (South Hampstead, the Swiss Cottage page's area), lifts and porters
 * (not in the sources).
 *
 * WHOSE WORDS. From what he approved: the wardrobe guide's lines on
 * wardrobes either side of a chimney breast ("Alcoves are a natural place for
 * bedroom storage, but the recess itself may not be deep enough for clothes
 * on conventional hangers. A hanging wardrobe will often need to project
 * beyond the chimney breast into the room..."), on interior lighting, and
 * its answer on built-in against freestanding ("scribed" made "cut to fit
 * the wall", the protocol's plain form); the walk-in page's line on
 * materials, colours and finishes; the alcove guide's lines on the whole
 * wall and on cupboards with shelving above; the guarantee; the fitted
 * wardrobe, walk-in and alcove price lines as approved on Hampstead. One
 * conduct line: parking, deliveries and access agreed before installation,
 * as on the other area pages. Everything else is new copy for him to check.
 *
 * CHECKED 17 Nov by three independent reviewers (copy rules, facts against
 * the facts file and approved text, the swap test against the eight other
 * area pages). Changed on their findings: the FAQ question without
 * "cheaper"; "landmarks rather than homes" (the listed firemen's cottages
 * are homes) became "nearly all the houses and blocks here are unlisted";
 * the ssp-044 alt counts no drawers (three are in frame) and the ssp-042 alt
 * names cubbies, not shoes; the Greek Streets drawing lost the diagonal
 * glazing the source gives for Gondar Gardens; the "terraces" heading now
 * says the Greek Streets and west of West End Lane; the Key flats line is
 * dated 2011; the lease line is advice, not a claim about Camden; the street
 * door line and the spare-room claim went; his projection lines are back
 * word for word; conservation area, mansion block, leasehold, listed and the
 * columns' tops are explained once; the lede and description no longer
 * share Highgate's wording.
 *
 * Street and building names are Camden's appraisal's, describing the homes.
 * None is a place we claim to have worked. No review names West Hampstead.
 *
 * FAQ sources, all three new, none on another area page: "How do you light
 * a walk-in wardrobe?" (Google People also ask, walk in wardrobe lighting:
 * "How to light a walk-in wardrobe?", made a whole question), "What looks
 * good in an alcove?" and "Does a built-in wardrobe cost more than a
 * freestanding one?" (People also ask, alcove wardrobes: "Is it cheaper to
 * build or buy a wardrobe?", reworded without "cheaper", the area rules
 * item 10); UK, English, desktop, 17 Nov 2026.
 * The other questions in those boxes were costs, design trends, sizes (not
 * confirmed by him) or rules of thumb with no fixed meaning. Answers from
 * what he approved: the wardrobe guide's lighting section and its
 * freestanding answer, and the alcove guide's cupboards-with-shelving lines.
 */
import AreaPage from "../../components/AreaPage.astro";
import Wrap from "../../components/ds/Wrap.astro";
import SectionHead from "../../components/ds/SectionHead.astro";
import Faq from "../../components/ds/Faq.astro";
import Link from "../../components/ds/Link.astro";
import AreaSide from "../../components/AreaSide.astro";
import PriceCards from "../../components/PriceCards.astro";
import Ask from "../../components/Ask.astro";

import { AREAS } from "../../data/areas";

const area = AREAS.find((a) => a.slug === "areas/west-hampstead")!;

const PRICES = [
  {
    art: "fitted-wardrobes",
    name: "Fitted wardrobes",
    href: "/fitted-wardrobes/",
    price: "From £2,300 for a 2-door run up to 1.2m in melamine, and from £4,000 at 4 doors.",
    moves: "Width, finish, internal drawers, integrated lighting and more complex designs move the figure.",
  },
  {
    art: "walk-in-wardrobes",
    name: "Walk-in wardrobes",
    href: "/walk-in-wardrobes/",
    price: "Typically from around £5,000.",
    moves: "The size of the room, number of fitted runs, internal layout, materials and finishes, and integrated lighting move the figure.",
  },
  {
    art: "alcove-units",
    name: "Alcove units",
    href: "/alcove-units/",
    price: "From £3,300 for a pair in melamine, and from £6,000 in oak veneer.",
    moves: "Finish, shelving detail, drawers and lighting move the figure. It assumes base cupboards with shelving over, up to about 1m per alcove.",
  },
];
const P2 = "margin-top:var(--space-10)";
const TEXT = "max-width:var(--measure)";
const SECTION = "padding-bottom:var(--section-y)";
const EXT = { target: "_blank", rel: "noopener" };
---

<AreaPage area={area}>
  <Fragment slot="lede">
    <p>
      We design and install <Link href="/fitted-wardrobes/">fitted wardrobes</Link>, <Link href="/walk-in-wardrobes/">walk-in wardrobes</Link> and <Link href="/alcove-units/">alcove units</Link> for homes in West Hampstead, around West End Lane and Fortune Green. If your mansion flat or red-brick house has alcoves beside a chimney breast or a loft room, we'll plan the storage around them.
    </p>
  </Fragment>

  <Wrap style={SECTION}>
    <SectionHead meta="Mansion flats" level={4}>Fitted wardrobes for mansion flats in West Hampstead</SectionHead>
    <AreaSide drawing="gabled-mansion-block">
      <p>
        Mansion flats stand above the shops on West End Lane and around Cannon Hill and Mill Lane. A hinged <Link href="/fitted-wardrobes/">wardrobe</Link> is often around 600 to 650 mm deep, so in a shallow alcove we'll bring it forward of the chimney breast. We finish its exposed sides to match the doors, which you can have in Shaker or handleless style.
      </p>
    </AreaSide>
  </Wrap>

  <Wrap style={SECTION}>
    <SectionHead meta="Edwardian houses" level={4}>Walk-in wardrobes for Edwardian houses in West Hampstead</SectionHead>
    <AreaSide drawing="edwardian-balcony-house">
      <p>
        East of West End Lane, Lymington Road, Fawley Road and Crediton Hill have large semi-detached and detached Edwardian houses. If you have a box room or spare room of at least <Ask q="west-hampstead-walk-in-size">smallest room size, to confirm</Ask>, we'll design a <Link href="/walk-in-wardrobes/">walk-in wardrobe</Link> around it. We plan the hanging space, drawers, shoe storage and shelves together, in finishes that go with your flooring and furniture.
      </p>
    </AreaSide>
  </Wrap>

  <Wrap style={SECTION}>
    <SectionHead meta="Terraced houses" level={4}>Alcove units for living rooms in West Hampstead's terraced houses</SectionHead>
    <AreaSide drawing="greek-street-bays">
      <p>
        West of West End Lane, streets such as Inglewood Road and Agamemnon Road are lined with terraced houses. For books in your living room <Link href="/alcove-units/">alcoves</Link>, we'll start with gaps of around 250 to 350 mm between shelves. Taller display pieces need around 350 to 450 mm or more.
      </p>
    </AreaSide>
  </Wrap>

  <Wrap style={SECTION}>
    <SectionHead meta="Loft rooms" level={4}>Fitted wardrobes under sloping ceilings in West Hampstead</SectionHead>
    <div style={TEXT}>
      <p>
        Houses on the north side of Lyncroft Gardens have rooms in the roof. In a loft room, we'll put the hanging space at the tallest point and drawers or cupboards under the low side. We'll size each door so it opens fully under the slope.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Wrap>

  <Wrap style={SECTION}>
    <SectionHead meta="Listed homes" level={4}>Fitted furniture for listed West Hampstead homes</SectionHead>
    <div style={TEXT}>
      <p>
        Before you plan fitted wardrobes or shelving, you can check whether your home is listed on <Link href="https://www.camden.gov.uk/listed-buildings" {...EXT}>Camden Council's listed buildings page</Link>. If it is, ask the council before work starts whether the joinery needs listed building consent. It matters most if fitting the joinery means removing or changing original skirting or wall panelling.
      </p>
      <p style={P2}>
        If the room has an original cornice, we can stop a wardrobe below it so the moulding stays in view.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Wrap>

  <Wrap style={SECTION}>
    <SectionHead meta="Access" level={4}>Parking, lifts and access on fitting days in West Hampstead</SectionHead>
    <div style={TEXT}>
      <p>
        Before fitting day, we'll agree with you where we can unload and park. You can find your parking zone by street or postcode on <Link href="https://www.camden.gov.uk/controlled-parking-zones" {...EXT}>Camden Council's controlled parking zones page</Link>. If a permit is needed, we'll decide together who applies for a <Link href="https://www.camden.gov.uk/trade-permits" {...EXT}>Camden trade permit</Link>.
      </p>
      <p style={P2}>
        In a mansion block, we'll ask you about the lift, the stairs and any rules on deliveries. Where the building needs it, we can work with the managing agent and send our insurance and safety documents. If the lift or stairs are narrow, the furniture can come in sections and be assembled in the room.
      </p>
      <p style={P2}>
        We normally work between 8am and 5pm on weekdays, and we can adjust the hours to suit you and the building. On fitting day, we protect the floors and stairs on the way in before any materials arrive.
      </p>
    </div>
  </Wrap>

  <Wrap style={SECTION}>
    <SectionHead meta="Guide prices" level={4}>What fitted wardrobes, walk-ins and alcove units cost in West Hampstead</SectionHead>
    <AreaSide>
      <p>Every figure here is a starting price, and a larger or more detailed piece costs more.</p>
      <p style={P2}>
        The guarantee covers installation and workmanship for 2 years and the structure of the joinery for 10 years. Hardware is on the manufacturer's warranty, and doors and drawers are adjusted in the first 2 years. Our <Link href="/fitted-wardrobe-cost/">cost guide</Link> shows what moves the price of a fitted wardrobe.
      </p>
      <PriceCards slot="side" items={PRICES} />
    </AreaSide>
  </Wrap>

  <Wrap style={SECTION}>
    <SectionHead meta="Questions" scribe>Asked before enquiring</SectionHead>
    <div style="margin-top:var(--space-12);max-width:var(--measure);border-bottom:var(--rule-hair)">
      <Faq q="How do you light a walk-in wardrobe?">
        We can fit vertical light strips near the front of each hanging section and lights under the shelves for folded clothes. We position them so the clothes and shelves don't block the light. Before fitting, we'll agree the light colour with you, using samples beside the interior finish.
      </Faq>
      <Faq q="What looks good in an alcove?">
        Low cupboards with open shelves above give you hidden storage and space for books and framed pictures. A taller cupboard hides more, while a lower one leaves more room for shelves. For a detailed design, we can draw both alcoves and the fireplace together so you see the whole wall.
      </Faq>
      <Faq q="Does a built-in wardrobe cost more than a freestanding one?">
        A built-in wardrobe usually costs more to buy than a freestanding or flat-pack one. It's designed for your room, so it can use the full height and width of the wall, alcoves and sloping ceilings included. With normal use and care, we expect ours to last well beyond 10 years.
      </Faq>
    </div>
  </Wrap>
</AreaPage>
